      "text": "The problem involves a three-dimensional geometry, where we have a sphere inscribed in a cube and we need to find the volume of the sphere. The sphere is the largest possible sphere that can fit inside the cube, so the diameter of the sphere is equal to the edge length of the cube. Therefore, the formula for the volume of a sphere gives us the answer.\n\nWe know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$. We are given the edge length of the cube, which is 6 inches. The diameter of the sphere will be the same as the edge length of the cube, so the diameter of the sphere is 6 inches. We can then calculate the radius of the sphere:\n\n$$\\text{The radius of the sphere} = \\frac{\\text{The diameter of the sphere}}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\text{ inches}$$\n\nNow that we know the diameter, we can substitute it into our formula to calculate the volume of the sphere:\n\n$$\\text{Volume of the sphere} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3)^3$$\n\nSimplifying the volume expression gives us:\n\n$$\\text{Volume of the sphere} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi(27) = 36\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the inscribed sphere is $\\boxed{36\\pi}$ cubic inches.",
